nodecode-permission-canvas is a rewrite of TANGBUDU's TANGBUDU/permission-canvas:

  https://github.com/TANGBUDU/permission-canvas
  commit 3a3086066051f5cc7a6ee52c20d62395b814da3f
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 TANGBUDU (LICENSE)

The original was a Hermes Agent computer-use backend: it wrapped a desktop
driver, kept a policy file of per-control rules (green operate, blue read
only, yellow one approval, black invisible and masked), matched a captured
accessibility tree against those rules, checked every dispatched input, and
redacted the hidden controls out of the screenshot and the tree before the
model saw them. Its policy engine is policy.lisp here, rule for rule: the
same four levels with the same ranks, the same scope fields (app, role,
label, label_regex, window_title_regex, pid, window_id), the same
"the most restrictive match wins", the same fail-closed reading of a scope
field the driver did not report (with the same exception for a green rule),
the same unidentified level for an unlabeled control in a restricted
window, the same concealed-by-overlap and concealed-by-descent withholding,
the same title privacy pattern, the same fixed content-free messages, and
the same one-operation approval keyed on the action, the control's
fingerprint, the arguments and a digest of the whole policy. Its approval
store is approvals.json here: pending, approved once, denied - the model
never approves itself.

What the rewrite adds is the boundary the cell has instead of a driver
wrapper: the model asks permission_canvas_check before it acts and
permission_canvas_observe before it looks, and a person writes the rules
and grants one exact operation with /permission-canvas. What it left out is
what needed the original's runtime and a Windows desktop: the cua-driver
MCP transport and its screenshot capture, the Tkinter Canvas panel (its
tree outline, its window picker and its private preview), the accessibility
helper, the focus-provenance model that refused typing after an unproven
focus change, the browser occlusion retry, and the OS file lock that paused
the agent while the panel was open - a pause is an explicit command here
instead. Nothing of the original is bundled and nothing of it runs: the
cell is Lisp over files in one folder, with no key, no network and no
subprocess.
